Optimizing complex stencil computations for GPU performance remains a key challenge in scientific computing, particularly for high-order CFD methods like the 7th-order WENO scheme. Such real-world stencils are characterized by wide templates, multi-variable data access, and hybrid cell/face-centered operations, which collectively lead to inefficient memory access, suboptimal thread utilization, and excessive data movement on GPU architectures. This results in a significant performance gap between optimized standard stencils and production-grade simulation codes. To address this challenge, WindStencil is introduced, a stencil computing system specifically designed for such complex patterns. The system consists of three synergistic components: (1) a Block-based Fully Fused 3D WENO Kernel that merges multiple computational steps into a single kernel to drastically reduce global memory traffic while ensuring high memory access efficiency; (2) a Thread Scheduling Strategy that reorganizes work using a plane-scanning approach to better map cell/face-center tasks onto GPU threads; and (3) a Three-level Prefetching Scheme that creates a fine-grained pipeline to hide memory latency. Evaluation results demonstrate that WindStencil delivers breakthrough single-GPU performance for a demanding 7th-order 3D25P WENO stencil. On an AMD MI60 GPU, the system achieves 37.2% of peak floating-point performance, and 30.8% on AMD MI200 series GPUs. These results offer promising advancements for practical high-order CFD simulations, demonstrating that complex production stencils can achieve much higher efficiency on modern GPU hardware than previously possible.